## In 30 seconds

Mark contracts with **@RegistrationPoint**, implementations with **@Register**, startup components with **@BootComponent**. The compile-time processor generates a **ModuleStarter** and SPI entry; JPMS modules `provides ModuleStarter with …GeneratedStarter`.

Quick Start
---------------------

Modularization uses **core** plus the compile-time **processor** artifact. Annotate your module; the processor generates a `ModuleStarter` and SPI file.

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.jrebirth.af</groupId>
    <artifactId>core</artifactId>
    <version>${jrebirth.version}</version>
</dependency>

<!-- Annotation processor (compiler plugin path) -->
<path>
    <groupId>org.jrebirth.af</groupId>
    <artifactId>processor</artifactId>
    <version>${jrebirth.version}</version>
    <classifier>4ide</classifier>
</path>
```

**Minimal example — self-describing module (Todos showcase):**

```java
@RegistrationPoint
public interface TodoService extends Service {
    boolean doAdd(StringProperty text, Wave wave);
}

@Register(value = TodoService.class)
public class TodoServiceImpl extends DefaultService implements TodoService {
    @Override
    public boolean doAdd(StringProperty text, Wave wave) {
        todoList.addTodo(Todo.of().text(text.getValue()).done(false));
        return true;
    }
}
```

In `module-info.java`, declare `provides ModuleStarter with …TodosModuleStarter;` — the starter class is generated at compile time.


Modularization
====================

Module overview
---------------------

JRebirth supports modular applications through a combination of **JPMS** (`module-info.java`) and a **compile-time annotation processor** that generates bootstrap code for each module.

A module is typically packaged as a separate JAR that contributes Commands, Services, Models, or Behaviors to the host application. Thanks to the annotation-driven approach, modules are self-describing: the host application does not need to know implementation details &mdash; it only depends on interfaces.

The key moving parts are:

* **@RegistrationPoint** &mdash; marks an interface as an extensible contract.
* **@Register** &mdash; binds an implementation to a registration point.
* **@BootComponent** &mdash; declares a component to instantiate at startup.
* **@Preload** &mdash; forces a class to be loaded early (e.g. to register WaveTypes).
* **ComponentProcessor** &mdash; the annotation processor that scans the above and generates a **ModuleStarter** class plus the SPI service file.
* **ModuleStarter / AbstractModuleStarter** &mdash; the runtime SPI that the engine calls during boot.

