Component Registration
-----------------------

Each extensible component shall be registered using one **@RegistrationPoint** and one or many **@Register** declarations.

### @RegistrationPoint ###

Place this annotation on an **interface** that extends `Component` (e.g. a Service or Model contract). It declares that this type can have pluggable implementations provided by any module.

* `exclusive` (default `true`) &mdash; when true, only the single highest-priority implementation is resolved at runtime. Set to `false` to allow multiple co-existing implementations.
* `reverse` (default `false`) &mdash; when true, lower-priority implementations are returned first.


```java
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.SOURCE)
@Documented
public @interface RegistrationPoint {

    boolean exclusive() default true;

    boolean reverse() default false;
}
```

### @Register ###

Place this annotation on an **implementation class** to bind it to its registration-point interface.

* `value` &mdash; the interface class being implemented.
* `priority` &mdash; a `PriorityLevel` enum (`Lowest`, `Lower`, `Normal`, `High`, `Highest`, &hellip;) that determines selection order.
* `weight` &mdash; an additional integer (0&ndash;999) for fine-grained ordering within the same priority level.


```java
/**
 * The Interface Register.
 */
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.SOURCE)
@Documented
public @interface Register {

    /**
     * Value.
     *
     * @return the class
     */
    Class<?> value() default Class.class;

    /**
     * Priority.
     *
     * @return the registration priority
     */
    PriorityLevel priority() default PriorityLevel.None;

    /**
     * Additional Weight to perform priority calculation.
     *
     * Shall be strictly lower than 1000
     *
     * @return the registration priority
     */
    int weight() default 0;

}
```

### Example ###

The Todos showcase defines a `TodoService` contract and its implementation:


```java
@RegistrationPoint
public interface TodoService extends Service {

    WaveType DO_ADD = waveType("ADD")
        .items(TODO_STRING)
        .returnItem(JRebirthItems.booleanItem)
        .returnAction("ADDED");

    boolean doAdd(final StringProperty text, final Wave wave);
}
```

```java
@Register(value = TodoService.class)
public class TodoServiceImpl extends DefaultService implements TodoService {

    private final TodoList todoList = new TodoList();

    @Override
    public boolean doAdd(final StringProperty text, final Wave wave) {
        todoList.addTodo(Todo.of().text(text.getValue()).done(false));
        return true;
    }
}
```


@BootComponent
-----------------------

Annotate a component class with **@BootComponent** to have it automatically instantiated when the module starts. The annotation processor adds a `bootComponent(MyComponent.class)` call to the generated `ModuleStarter.start()` method.

This is useful for components that must be alive from the very beginning (e.g. a global service that listens for waves at application startup).


```java
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.SOURCE)
@Documented
public @interface BootComponent {

}
```


@Preload
-----------------------

The **@Preload** annotation forces a class to be loaded by the classloader early in the module startup sequence. This triggers static initializers, which is particularly useful for classes that define **WaveType** constants or register resources via JRebirth builders.

When a class annotated with `@Preload` is detected, the processor generates a `preloadClass(MyClass.class)` call in the starter.


```java
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Inherited
@Documented
public @interface Preload {

}
```


ModuleStarter
-----------------------

**ModuleStarter** is the SPI interface that the JRebirth engine discovers and invokes during application boot. Each module provides exactly one implementation.

The interface defines:

* `start()` &mdash; called by the engine to let the module register its components.
* `priority()` &mdash; controls the order in which modules are started (higher values start first, default is 0).


```java
@FunctionalInterface
public interface ModuleStarter {

    /**
     * This method will be called when all modules are started during application boot up.
     */
    void start();

    /**
     * Priority used to launch Modules according to custom order.
     * 
     * The higher priority value will be called first.
     * 
     * @return the module priority from 0 to Integer.MAX_VALUE.
     */
    default int priority() {
        return 0;
    }
}
```

The framework provides **AbstractModuleStarter** (in the `core` module) as the base class for generated starters. It offers helper methods:

* `define(interfaceClass, exclusive, reverse)` &mdash; declares a registration point.
* `register(interfaceClass, implClass, priority, weight)` &mdash; registers an implementation.
* `bootComponent(componentClass)` &mdash; retrieves a component from its facade to force instantiation.
* `preloadClass(objectClass)` &mdash; forces classloading of the given type.


Module Auto-loading
---------------------

To automate module management, JRebirth uses the **ComponentProcessor** annotation processor at compilation time.

### What the processor does ###

The `ComponentProcessor` (in the `processor` module) is registered for four annotation types: `@Register`, `@RegistrationPoint`, `@BootComponent`, and `@Preload`. During compilation it:

1. Scans all annotated elements in the current compilation unit.
2. Generates a **ModuleStarter implementation class** (extending `AbstractModuleStarter`) whose `start()` method calls `define()`, `register()`, `bootComponent()`, and `preloadClass()` for every annotated element found.
3. Generates the **SPI service file** at `META-INF/services/org.jrebirth.af.api.module.ModuleStarter` containing the fully-qualified name of the generated starter class.

The starter class name is derived from the Maven `groupId` and `artifactId` of the module (read from `pom.xml`), or from a `moduleStarterClass` property in `jrebirth.properties` if present.


```java
@Override
public Set<String> getSupportedAnnotationTypes() {
    return Set.of(
        Preload.class.getName(),
        BootComponent.class.getName(),
        Register.class.getName(),
        RegistrationPoint.class.getName());
}

@Override
public boolean process(final Set<? extends TypeElement> annotations,
                       final RoundEnvironment roundEnv) {
    if (roundEnv.processingOver()) {
        return false;
    }
    // collect @Register, @RegistrationPoint, @BootComponent, @Preload
    // → generate ModuleStarter + SPI file
    return true;
}
```

### Generated output example ###

For the Todos showcase, the processor generates `TodosModuleStarter`:


```java
public class TodosModuleStarter extends AbstractModuleStarter {

    @Override
    public void start() {
        define(TodoService.class, true, false);
        register(TodoService.class, TodoServiceImpl.class, PriorityLevel.Normal, 0);
        bootComponent(DialogDaemonCommand.class);  // example from another module
    }
}
```

Inspect `target/generated-sources/annotations/` after compilation for the actual generated class in your project.


JPMS Integration
---------------------

When using Java Platform Module System (`module-info.java`), each JRebirth module must:

* **`requires`** `org.jrebirth.af.api` and `org.jrebirth.af.core` (plus any other framework modules it depends on).
* **`exports`** packages containing public API types (interfaces, Models, Services) that other modules need.
* **`provides`** `org.jrebirth.af.api.module.ModuleStarter` **`with`** the generated starter class, so that `ServiceLoader` can discover it at runtime.

Here is an example from the Todos showcase:


```java
    provides org.jrebirth.af.api.module.ModuleStarter with org.jrebirth.af.showcase.todos.TodosModuleStarter;
```

Using `open module` simplifies reflection access (useful during development), but for production you can selectively `opens` packages to `org.jrebirth.af.core` instead.


